/* Original beginner explanations. Each row is tied to one existing lesson, not a generated template. */
const BEGINNER_RAW = `
foundation-0~A cell is a tiny living unit, like a muscle cell.|A tissue is an organized group of cells and the material around them.|An organ combines tissues to do a job; organs cooperate in systems.~Your stomach has a protective lining, muscles that mix food, and nerves that coordinate the mixing.~Cell → tissue → organ → system: each level adds cooperation.~An organ is not made of just one tissue.
foundation-1~A medical word often contains a body-part root and a word ending.|Cardio means heart; -itis means inflammation, the body's response to injury.|Putting word parts together gives a clue, but you must check the full meaning.~Hepatitis means inflammation of the liver. Viruses, some medicines, and other causes can produce it.~Learn the meaning before learning the spelling.~A disease name does not automatically reveal its cause.
foundation-2~Imagine a person standing upright, looking forward, with palms facing forward.|Directions describe that person's body, not your view of a picture.|A plane is an imaginary flat cut used to describe a section of the body.~Your nose is medial to your ears: it is closer to the body's middle line.~Anterior = front; posterior = back; superior = above; inferior = below.~The patient's right can be on the left side of your picture.
foundation-3~A membrane is the thin boundary around a cell.|Some substances cross it easily; others need protein channels or carriers.|Diffusion produces net movement down a concentration difference; osmosis is water movement across a selective membrane.~A tea bag shows dissolved particles spreading into water. It illustrates diffusion, but a living cell also controls many crossings.~Diffusion spreads particles; osmosis concerns water; active transport uses energy.~A membrane is selective, not a sealed brick wall.
foundation-4~Cells work best within suitable conditions, such as a limited temperature range.|Sensors detect a change and the body produces a response.|Negative feedback reduces the original change; it does not mean something bad.~When you become hot, sweating can remove heat as the sweat evaporates.~Change → detect → respond → reduce the change.~Homeostasis means active adjustment, not a body that never changes.
foundation-5~The heart pushes blood around two connected loops.|The lung loop picks up oxygen; the body loop delivers it to tissues.|Red blood cells carry most oxygen using a protein called hemoglobin.~A delivery van needs both parcels and a working road. Similarly, tissues need oxygen-carrying blood and enough blood flow.~Arteries go away from the heart; veins return toward it.~Artery does not mean oxygen-rich: pulmonary arteries go to the lungs with oxygen-poor blood.
foundation-6~Breathing brings fresh air into tiny lung air sacs called alveoli.|Oxygen crosses their thin walls into blood; carbon dioxide crosses out.|Blood flow then carries the exchanged gases elsewhere.~Opening a classroom window moves air, but delivery to each student is another step. Air entering lungs is only the first part of oxygen delivery.~Ventilation moves air; diffusion moves gases across a barrier; perfusion moves blood.~Breathing movement alone does not prove good gas exchange.
foundation-7~Digestion cuts large food molecules into smaller pieces.|Absorption moves those pieces through the gut wall into the body.|Cells use nutrients to build materials, store fuel, and transfer energy into ATP, a molecule used for cellular work.~Eating bread is not the same as delivering glucose to a muscle: digestion, absorption, transport, and cell use come in between.~Digestion breaks down; absorption takes in; metabolism uses or changes molecules.~Food is not converted directly into movement in the stomach.
foundation-8~Kidneys begin by filtering water and small dissolved substances from blood.|Their tiny tubes return much of the water and useful material to blood.|What remains, plus substances added to the tubes, leaves as urine.~Think of sorting a mixed bag and keeping useful items. The kidney's filter is only the first sorting stage.~Filter first, reclaim what is needed, excrete the remainder.~Filtered does not mean permanently lost.
foundation-9~A neuron is a nerve cell that sends messages.|A hormone is a chemical message released into blood.|A cell responds only if it has the right receptor, a protein that recognizes that message.~A radio receives many signals but responds to the station it is tuned to. Receptors give cells a kind of message selectivity.~Message + matching receptor → response.~A hormone does not make every cell do the same thing.
foundation-10~Skin and other barriers make entry difficult for microbes.|Fast defenses respond to common danger patterns; adaptive defenses learn more specific targets.|Vaccines expose the immune system to a safe form of a target or its instructions so it can prepare a response.~A practice drill helps a team respond later. Immune memory is biological preparation, not conscious remembering.~Barrier → response → memory.~Not every microbe is harmful, and protection is not always complete.
foundation-11~A symptom is something a person feels, such as pain.|A sign is something another person can observe or measure.|An investigation is a test used to answer a particular question.~Feeling feverish is a symptom; a thermometer reading is a measured sign. Both help interpretation.~Ask what the person reports, what is measured, and what question the test answers.~A test result alone is not the whole diagnosis.
foundation-12~Risk is the chance of an event in a stated group and time.|A risk factor changes the chance; it does not decide an individual's future.|Evidence updates what is likely, and uncertainty may remain.~If risk rises from 1 in 100 to 2 in 100, it doubles relatively but rises by 1 percentage point absolutely.~Always ask: out of how many, and over what time?~Two things occurring together does not prove one caused the other.
foundation-13~First explain the idea in ordinary words.|Then close the page and try to reproduce the explanation.|Check mistakes and return after a delay, when remembering takes some effort.~After studying circulation, draw the two loops from memory and then correct your drawing.~Understand → retrieve → check → revisit.~Recognizing a sentence while reading is easier than recalling its meaning without help.
anatomy-0~A surface needs protection, so epithelial tissue covers or lines it.|A moving wall needs muscle, and a supported structure needs connective tissue.|Nervous tissue coordinates these jobs using signals.~The gut needs all four: lining, support, movement, and control.~Name the job of each tissue before memorizing its appearance.~A whole organ is not the same as one tissue type.
anatomy-1~Nerves leave the spinal cord and join a network called the brachial plexus.|Branches travel to muscles and skin in the arm.|Damage can interrupt movement messages, sensation messages, or both.~If wrist-lifting muscles lose their signal, the wrist may droop. The radial nerve is an important route for that movement.~Follow the route: spinal roots → nerve network → muscle or skin.~One weak movement alone does not prove exactly where an injury lies.
anatomy-2~Muscles pull bones across joints to move a leg.|Other muscles keep the pelvis level while one foot is lifted.|Nerves coordinate the pull and return information about position.~Standing on one leg needs hip muscles to stop the unsupported side of the pelvis from dropping.~Quadriceps straighten the knee; hip abductors stabilize the pelvis.~Walking requires stability as well as forward movement.
anatomy-3~The chest, abdomen, and pelvis are spaces containing organs.|Tubes and blood vessels connect these organs along specific routes.|Knowing which structures are neighbors helps explain how a nearby problem can affect them.~Blood from much of the gut passes through the portal vein to the liver before returning to the heart.~Learn location, neighbors, route, and job together.~The portal vein is a blood vessel, not a tube carrying food.
anatomy-4~The brain sends movement instructions along nerve pathways.|Many movement fibers cross sides in the lower brainstem, called the medulla.|Fluid around the brain and spinal cord supports and cushions them.~A crossed pathway helps explain why an injury on one side of the brain can affect the opposite side of the body.~Trace the route and mark where it crosses.~Not every nerve pathway crosses at the same place.
anatomy-5~An embryo starts with cells that divide and become specialized.|Three early cell layers contribute to different tissues.|Histology studies tissues under a microscope; genetics studies inherited information.~Ectoderm contributes to skin's outer layer and much of the nervous system, despite their very different adult jobs.~Embryology = development; histology = tissues; genetics = inherited information.~Early layers are starting populations, not fully formed miniature organs.
physiology-0~Cell membranes separate fluids with different amounts of dissolved ions.|Transport proteins maintain these differences, sometimes using energy.|The differences help cells move substances and send electrical signals.~The sodium-potassium pump uses ATP to move three sodium ions out and two potassium ions in per cycle.~A gradient is a difference; maintaining it may require work.~Negative feedback reduces a disturbance; positive feedback amplifies it until a stopping event.
physiology-1~A nerve signal is a moving change in electrical voltage across a membrane.|At the muscle connection, the nerve releases acetylcholine, a chemical messenger.|The muscle releases calcium internally, allowing protein filaments to slide and shorten the muscle.~Pulling a rope shortens the distance between two points. Muscle filaments slide past each other; they do not each shrink.~Electrical signal → chemical message → calcium signal → contraction.~A nerve does not physically pull a muscle like a string.
physiology-2~Heart rate counts beats each minute.|Stroke volume is the amount pumped by one ventricle per beat.|Multiplying them gives cardiac output: volume pumped per minute.~70 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 4,900 mL/min, or 4.9 L/min. This is a teaching example, not a target for everyone.~Keep the units: beats cancel and volume per minute remains.~Blood pressure and blood flow are related but are not the same measurement.
physiology-3~Air brings oxygen to alveoli.|Thin walls allow gases to diffuse between air and nearby blood.|Good exchange needs both fresh air and enough blood passing those air sacs.~A station with trains but no passengers cannot transport many people. Likewise, ventilated air sacs with poor blood flow exchange little gas overall.~Match air supply with blood supply.~Normal air entry does not guarantee normal oxygen transfer.
physiology-4~Each nephron is a tiny kidney filtering and processing unit.|It adjusts water, salts, and acid-related substances in the filtered fluid.|ADH helps the final tubes reclaim water; aldosterone helps regulate sodium and potassium.~After water loss, retaining more water can make urine more concentrated.~Reabsorption returns to blood; secretion adds to the tube; excretion leaves the body.~Kidneys do much more than remove solid waste.
physiology-5~Digestive organs supply nutrients.|Endocrine glands release hormones into blood to coordinate their use.|Feedback between brain, glands, and target organs adjusts hormone release.~After a meal, insulin promotes storage and helps certain tissues take up glucose, reducing blood glucose.~A hormone needs a target cell with a suitable receptor.~Every hormone is not simply an on-off switch; effects depend on context and amount.
biochemistry-0~An enzyme helps a chemical reaction happen faster.|Its active site interacts with a starting molecule called a substrate.|A competing inhibitor occupies the same binding site and makes substrate binding less likely.~Like a shared workbench, access matters. In the ideal competitive model, enough substrate can still allow the same maximum reaction rate.~Vmax is maximum rate; Km is the substrate level at half that rate.~An enzyme is reused; it is not the fuel consumed by the reaction.
biochemistry-1~Glucose is a six-carbon sugar used as fuel.|Glycolysis splits it into smaller molecules in the cell fluid.|Further pathways can transfer more fuel energy into ATP.~A savings account and spending wallet are different: glycogen stores glucose units, while ATP supports immediate cellular work.~Glycolysis breaks glucose; glycogenesis stores it; gluconeogenesis makes new glucose.~ATP production involves several linked pathways, not one step from sugar to energy.
biochemistry-2~Fat mixes poorly with watery blood.|The body packages fats with proteins into transport particles called lipoproteins.|Different particles carry different mixtures to and from tissues.~Chylomicrons carry much of the fat absorbed from a meal, rather like packages with a water-compatible outer surface.~Transport particle and transported fat are different things.~LDL and HDL values are part of a wider risk picture, not complete verdicts on health.
biochemistry-3~Amino acids are building blocks of proteins and contain nitrogen.|When they are broken down, the nitrogen must be handled safely.|The liver converts much of it into urea, which kidneys remove.~A blocked assembly line may pile up starting material and run short of its product. Enzyme defects can do the same in a pathway.~Liver makes urea; kidneys excrete it.~An inherited pathway problem can cause both accumulation and shortage.
biochemistry-4~Vitamins and minerals are needed in smaller amounts than major food fuels.|Many help enzymes or support structures rather than supply energy directly.|Too little can impair function, while too much of some can also harm.~Vitamin C helps steps needed for strong collagen, a supporting protein in tissues.~A, D, E, K are fat-soluble vitamins; small requirement does not mean small importance.~A vitamin supplement is not a replacement for all the roles of food.
biochemistry-5~DNA stores instructions using a sequence of chemical units.|Transcription makes an RNA copy of selected information.|Translation uses RNA instructions to assemble a protein.~A library master copy, a working copy, and the built product illustrate DNA, RNA, and protein. The analogy does not cover every RNA function.~Replication copies DNA; transcription makes RNA; translation makes protein.~A laboratory result needs its units and context to mean anything useful.
pathology-0~Cells respond to extra demand by changing size, number, or type.|If stress exceeds their coping ability, injury develops.|Some injury can recover; severe injury can lead to cell death.~Exercise can enlarge muscle cells: this illustrates hypertrophy, an increase in size rather than number.~Hypertrophy = bigger cells; hyperplasia = more cells.~Adaptation is not automatically disease, and cell death has different mechanisms.
pathology-1~Injury triggers signals that change nearby blood vessels and attract defense cells.|These cells help contain threats and remove damaged material.|Repair replaces cells or lays down scar tissue, depending on the damage and tissue.~A scraped knee becomes inflamed, then closes; a deeper injury may leave a lasting scar.~Inflammation responds to injury; repair restores continuity.~Inflammation can happen without infection.
pathology-2~Blood normally stays fluid while moving inside vessels.|Vessel injury, slow or abnormal flow, and a greater tendency to clot can promote a clot.|The immune system can also damage tissue if its response targets the wrong thing or is excessive.~A clot formed in a leg vein can break free and travel; the traveling blockage is an embolus.~Thrombus forms in place; embolus travels.~An embolus need not be a blood clot; other material can also travel.
pathology-3~Cells normally obey controls on growth.|Cancer develops when changes allow abnormal growth and invasion of surrounding tissue.|Some cancer cells travel and grow elsewhere: this is metastasis.~Grade asks how abnormal the cells look; stage asks how far disease has extended.~Grade = cell features; stage = extent.~A benign growth does not metastasize, but its location or size can still cause problems.
pathology-4~Red cells carry oxygen, white cells help defense, and platelets help stop bleeding.|Different blood disorders disrupt different jobs.|Anemia means reduced hemoglobin for the relevant reference range, so oxygen-carrying capacity falls.~Small red cells suggest one group of causes; large cells suggest another. Cell size is a clue, not the final answer.~Ask which blood component and which job are affected.~Anemia is not always caused by lack of iron.
pathology-5~Start with the normal job of an organ.|A structural change can disturb that job in a predictable way.|Similar symptoms can still result from different underlying changes.~If kidney filters leak much protein into urine, less protein may remain in blood and swelling can develop through several linked mechanisms.~Structure → function → symptom.~An organ-level pattern such as nephrotic syndrome is not one unique cause.
pharmacology-0~A medicine must reach the body, move to a target, and eventually be removed.|Pharmacokinetics describes that journey.|Pharmacodynamics describes what happens when the medicine interacts with its target.~A drug needing fewer milligrams for an effect is more potent in that comparison; it need not achieve a greater maximum effect.~Kinetics = body handles drug; dynamics = drug affects body.~More potent does not automatically mean more useful or safer.
pharmacology-1~Automatic nerves help control heart, gut, glands, and airways.|They use chemical messages recognized by different receptor types.|Medicines can activate or block these receptors and change organ activity.~Activating beta-2 receptors can relax airway muscle and widen air passages.~The receptor type helps predict the response.~Receptor selectivity is relative; one drug may affect more targets as exposure increases.
pharmacology-2~Blood pressure depends partly on pumping, vessel resistance, and fluid balance.|Different drug groups act on different parts of this system.|A diuretic changes kidney salt handling, which can increase water loss in urine.~Blocking salt reabsorption leaves more salt in kidney tubes, often carrying more water with it.~Connect drug target → organ change → intended effect.~Antiplatelets and anticoagulants act on different parts of clot formation.
pharmacology-3~Nerve cells communicate using chemicals at junctions.|Some drugs change these messages; others reduce chemicals involved in pain and inflammation.|The same mechanism can contribute to both useful and unwanted effects.~NSAIDs reduce cyclooxygenase activity, which reduces production of prostaglandins, signaling molecules involved in pain and other body functions.~Learn target, useful effect, and unwanted effect together.~Pain relief, sleep, muscle relaxation, and unconsciousness are different effects.
pharmacology-4~Bacteria have structures and processes that differ from human cells.|Antibiotics target some of these differences.|Resistant bacteria survive a drug that would otherwise affect them and may become more common.~A bacterium that destroys an antibiotic before it reaches its target can survive exposure.~Target → drug action → possible resistance mechanism.~Antibiotics do not act on viruses in the same way they act on bacteria.
pharmacology-5~A drug changes a particular pathway, not a whole organ indiscriminately.|Acid-suppressing drugs act on acid production; airway drugs may act on muscle or inflammation.|Knowing the target explains why two drugs can help different parts of one problem.~A bronchodilator widens an airway; an inhaled corticosteroid reduces inflammation. These are different jobs.~Match the drug's mechanism to the process being changed.~Drugs used for the same illness are not automatically interchangeable.
microbiology-0~Bacteria are cells; viruses use a host cell to reproduce.|A sample must come from a useful location and be tested appropriately.|Different tests look for organisms, their molecules, or the body's response.~A culture tries to grow an organism; a nucleic-acid test looks for selected genetic material.~Sample → suitable method → contextual interpretation.~A positive result does not always mean that organism caused the illness.
microbiology-1~Innate defenses respond quickly to broad danger patterns.|Adaptive defenses recognize more specific targets called antigens.|B cells can become antibody-producing cells; T cells coordinate responses or act on target cells.~A matching antibody binds a particular target, rather like a shaped connector, although immune binding is more flexible than a lock.~B cells can make antibodies; T cells have several cell-based roles.~Immunity is teamwork, not antibodies alone.
microbiology-2~Bacteria differ in shape, grouping, cell wall, and growth needs.|These differences help organize identification.|Some cause damage by invading tissues; others release harmful substances called toxins.~Clusters and chains help describe bacteria seen under a microscope, but further testing may be needed to identify them.~Describe before identifying.~A bacterial shape alone does not determine the full diagnosis.
microbiology-3~A virus enters a suitable cell and uses cellular machinery to make copies.|New virus particles can then infect more cells.|Some viruses persist with limited activity and can reactivate later.~A dormant set of instructions is a rough analogy for latency; the viral material persists even without continuous obvious illness.~Entry → copying → release; persistence may follow.~No symptoms does not always mean no virus remains.
microbiology-4~Fungi include yeasts and molds; parasites live at a host's expense.|Some parasites pass through different hosts or body tissues during development.|A vector carries an infectious agent between hosts.~A mosquito can transmit malaria parasites; the mosquito and parasite are different organisms.~Separate agent, vector, host, and life-cycle stage.~All microscopic infections are not bacterial.
microbiology-5~Infection prevention interrupts routes by which microbes spread.|Cleaning removes material; disinfection reduces many harmful microbes.|Sterilization is a process intended to eliminate all microbial life, including resistant bacterial spores.~A visibly clean instrument is not necessarily sterile: appearance cannot establish absence of microbes.~Clean, disinfect, and sterilize describe different goals.~Resistance develops in microbes, not because a person's body becomes immune to antibiotics.
forensic-0~A person needs understandable information to make a meaningful choice.|Capacity concerns ability to make the relevant decision; voluntariness means freedom from pressure.|Confidentiality protects private information within applicable professional and legal rules.~A signed form alone does not show that someone understood the discussion.~Information + understanding + voluntary choice matter.~Consent is a decision-making process, not just a signature.
forensic-1~Evidence must be collected and documented so others can assess its reliability.|Chain of custody records who handled an item and when.|An observation is what was found; an inference is what that finding may mean.~Recording a wound's dimensions is an observation. Suggesting its cause is an interpretation that needs supporting evidence.~Keep finding, handling record, and interpretation distinct.~One clue does not automatically establish identity or cause.
forensic-2~After death, circulation stops and the body undergoes physical and chemical changes.|Cooling, settling of blood, and muscle stiffening occur through different processes.|Temperature and other conditions affect how these changes develop.~The same cooling observation can have different meanings in a cold room and a warm outdoor setting.~Algor = cooling; livor = blood settling; rigor = stiffness.~One postmortem sign cannot give an exact universal time of death.
forensic-3~Force can scrape, bruise, tear, or cut tissue.|Each describes a different pattern of damage.|A careful description comes before any conclusion about how it happened.~An abrasion affects the surface; a contusion involves bleeding beneath a surface; a laceration is a tear.~Describe location, size, shape, and surrounding findings.~Every open wound is not a clean cut from a sharp object.
forensic-4~Cells need a continuous supply of oxygen.|Delivery can fail through problems with air entry, gas exchange, blood transport, or circulation.|A cause must be assessed using the whole set of findings.~Small pinpoint bleeding spots can occur in more than one circumstance, so they are not a unique label for one cause.~Follow the oxygen pathway to understand possible failure points.~A nonspecific sign cannot establish a specific cause alone.
forensic-5~A substance's effect depends on how much enters, how it enters, and when.|Some poisons produce recognizable groups of effects called toxidromes.|These patterns guide professional assessment but are not definitive by themselves.~Organophosphates inhibit the enzyme that normally clears acetylcholine, so its signaling can become excessive.~Exposure → absorption → target effect.~A familiar symptom pattern is a clue, not a safe basis for self-treatment.
community-0~Epidemiology studies how health events are distributed across populations.|Incidence counts new cases over time; prevalence describes existing cases at a stated time or period.|Study designs compare groups in different ways, affecting which conclusions can reasonably be drawn.~A school with 20 existing cases on Monday has a different count from the 3 new cases arising during the next week. Existing and new cases answer different questions.~Prevalence = existing cases; incidence = new cases over time. Define the population for both.~Association alone is not proof of causation.
community-1~Data summarize observations, but different summaries answer different questions.|The mean adds values and divides by their count; the median is the middle ordered value.|Variation tells you how spread out the observations are.~For 2, 3, 4, 5, 100, the median is 4 while the mean is 22.8. One extreme value pulls the mean upward.~Read the center and the spread together.~A single average does not describe everyone in a group.
community-2~A screening test looks for possible disease in a target population.|Sensitivity counts positives among people with disease; specificity counts negatives among those without it.|The meaning of a positive result also depends on how common disease is in the tested group.~Among 100 people with disease, 90 positive results give 90% sensitivity. This says nothing yet about false positives among people without disease.~Always name the denominator.~High sensitivity does not mean every positive result is a true case.
community-3~Prevention can act before disease, detect it early, or reduce its consequences.|Population measures can reduce exposure for many people at once.|Control programs need monitoring to see whether they work.~Safe drinking water can reduce exposure before infection happens; rehabilitation helps after illness or injury has already occurred.~Primary prevents onset; secondary detects early; tertiary limits consequences.~Prevention is broader than vaccination alone.
community-4~Health needs change with pregnancy, childhood, and population age structure.|Nutrition measures need age and context.|Demography describes population size, composition, births, deaths, and movement.~Ten infant deaths have different population meanings among 100 births and among 100,000 births.~Counts need suitable denominators and time periods.~A larger number of events does not automatically mean a higher rate.
community-5~Health is shaped by water, housing, work, income, and access to care.|A workplace hazard is a potential source of harm; actual risk depends on exposure and other factors.|Health systems organize prevention, treatment, and support.~Removing a harmful exposure at its source protects people differently from relying only on each worker's behavior.~Look at the environment as well as the individual.~Health outcomes cannot always be explained by personal choices alone.
medicine-0~Clinical reasoning compares possible explanations with timing, symptoms, signs, and tests.|Urgent threats are assessed alongside the search for a cause.|Shock means circulation is failing to supply tissues adequately: the problem may involve too little circulating volume, a weak pump, abnormal distribution, or blocked flow.~A working water supply needs enough water, a functioning pump, and usable pipes. Each can fail differently; the shared result is inadequate delivery.~Shock concerns tissue supply. Identify the urgent problem, then distinguish its mechanism.~The first plausible explanation is not automatically correct.
medicine-1~The heart must fill with blood and then eject it effectively.|Ejection fraction is the percentage of a ventricle's filled volume pushed out per beat.|Heart failure can occur even with a preserved ejection fraction, for example when filling is impaired and pressures are raised.~Ejecting 60% of 50 mL moves 30 mL; ejecting 60% of 100 mL moves 60 mL. The same percentage does not mean the same volume. These are arithmetic examples, not diagnostic cutoffs.~A percentage is only part of the story: filling, pressure, output, and symptoms matter.~Heart failure does not mean the heart has stopped beating.
medicine-2~Air must pass through airways, reach air sacs, and exchange gases with blood.|Obstructive disease particularly limits airflow when breathing out; restrictive patterns involve reduced lung volumes.|Symptoms can overlap because several mechanisms reduce effective breathing.~A narrowed tube resists airflow; a container that cannot expand holds less air. These illustrate obstruction and restriction, although real lung diseases can overlap.~Obstruction limits flow; restriction limits volume.~Breathlessness is not one disease, and its cause need not be in the lungs.
medicine-3~The gut processes food, the liver handles absorbed substances, and kidneys regulate blood composition.|Kidney function can decline because too little blood arrives, kidney tissue is damaged, or urine outflow is blocked.|These categories are called prerenal, intrinsic renal, and postrenal, respectively.~A filter system can fail at its supply pipe, within its filter, or at its outlet. Urinary obstruction is an outlet problem, so it belongs to the postrenal category.~Prerenal = before the kidney; intrinsic = within; postrenal = after it in the urine pathway.~One abnormal blood result does not by itself identify the cause.
medicine-4~Hormone and immune disorders can affect several organs.|Insulin helps coordinate glucose handling; insulin resistance means tissues respond less effectively to it.|In type 2 diabetes, insulin-producing beta cells also fail to supply enough insulin for the body's needs, allowing glucose regulation to deteriorate.~A weaker response to a message requires a stronger signal. If the sender cannot provide enough, the intended result becomes harder to achieve.~Type 2 diabetes involves both reduced insulin response and inadequate beta-cell function.~Type 2 diabetes is not simply a complete absence of insulin, and it is not explained by one food alone.
medicine-5~Brain and nerve problems are interpreted through which functions change and how quickly.|Meningitis mainly involves the coverings of the brain and spinal cord; encephalitis involves brain tissue itself.|Sepsis occurs when an abnormal response to infection causes life-threatening organ dysfunction, meaning organs cannot carry out their jobs adequately.~An infection and fever do not automatically mean sepsis. The crucial added consequence is dangerous organ dysfunction from the body's disordered response.~Locate neurological changes; distinguish infection itself from infection with organ failure.~Sepsis is not simply the presence of microbes in blood.
surgery-0~A wound breaks normal tissue continuity.|Healing needs blood supply, suitable conditions, and coordinated repair.|Care before, during, and after an operation influences recovery.~Two similar cuts may heal differently if one has poor blood supply or infection.~Think preparation → procedure → recovery.~The operation itself is only one part of surgical care.
surgery-1~Shock means circulation is not meeting tissue needs adequately.|Blood loss, pump failure, and abnormal vessel behavior are different routes to shock.|Burns can also disturb the skin barrier and fluid balance.~A water system can fail because the tank is empty, the pump fails, or distribution changes; circulation has similar categories but is more complex.~Ask whether tissues receive enough flow and oxygen.~Shock in medicine is not simply emotional surprise.
surgery-2~The gut is a moving tube carrying contents forward.|A blockage causes material and gas to accumulate upstream.|Pressure, stretching, inflammation, or reduced blood supply can cause pain and complications.~A traffic jam backs up vehicles behind a blockage; intestinal obstruction similarly causes upstream accumulation.~Blockage → backup → pressure and functional disturbance.~Abdominal pain does not always require an operation.
surgery-3~Bile drains through ducts; digestive juices also travel through tubes.|A hernia is tissue protruding through a weak area or opening.|Trapped tissue becomes especially concerning when its blood supply is compromised.~A stuck hernia is incarcerated; one with impaired blood supply is strangulated.~Trapped and deprived of blood are different descriptions.~Not every hernia is strangulated.
surgery-4~A lump is a finding, not a diagnosis.|Examination, imaging, and sometimes tissue sampling answer different questions.|The results are interpreted together to identify the type and extent of disease.~Feeling a lump shows it is present; a tissue sample can reveal what kinds of cells it contains.~Location and feel are clues; tissue identity is a different question.~A lump is not automatically cancer.
surgery-5~Blood vessels and urinary tubes both depend on open flow paths.|Blockage can reduce supply downstream or build pressure upstream.|Urine production and bladder emptying are separate processes.~A full tank with a blocked outlet illustrates retention; an almost empty tank illustrates low production.~Produced but trapped differs from not produced.~Passing little urine does not by itself reveal which mechanism is present.
obgyn-0~The ovaries develop follicles containing egg cells.|Hormones coordinate egg release and changes in the uterine lining.|After ovulation, hormone patterns support a lining that could sustain pregnancy.~An LH surge helps trigger ovulation; progesterone then supports secretory changes in the lining.~Ovary events and uterus events belong on the same timeline.~Ovulation is not fixed to day 14 for everyone.
obgyn-1~Pregnancy requires the mother's body to support a developing fetus.|Antenatal means before birth; monitoring follows both maternal and fetal health.|Screening estimates the possibility of a condition and may lead to further tests.~A screening result can identify a need for closer assessment without confirming disease.~Timing in pregnancy changes how findings are interpreted.~A positive screening result is not always a diagnosis.
obgyn-2~Labor involves coordinated uterine muscle contractions.|The cervix opens, the baby is born, and then the placenta is delivered.|These events define the three stages of labor.~Dilation means opening; descent means moving downward. Both matter, but they describe different changes.~First: cervix opens. Second: birth. Third: placenta.~Labor progress cannot be understood from contraction pain alone.
obgyn-3~After birth, the uterus normally contracts to help limit bleeding.|Bleeding can arise from poor contraction, injury, retained tissue, or clotting problems.|Pregnancy-related high blood pressure can also involve other organs.~The four Ts organize bleeding causes: tone, trauma, tissue, and thrombin (clotting).~Use the four Ts to organize mechanisms, not as a treatment recipe.~Pregnancy-related risk does not end immediately after delivery.
obgyn-4~Contraception prevents pregnancy through different mechanisms.|Fertility depends on eggs, sperm, transport pathways, and other conditions.|Assessment and choices must reflect the person's goals and circumstances.~An open tube alone cannot ensure pregnancy if egg release or sperm function is impaired.~Consider both partners and the whole reproductive pathway.~Difficulty conceiving is not automatically a problem in only one partner.
obgyn-5~The uterus, cervix, and ovaries are different structures with different diseases.|A benign growth, inflammation, and cancer are different processes.|Persistent infection with certain HPV types contributes to most cervical cancers.~A fibroid is a benign growth of uterine muscle; endometriosis involves tissue resembling uterine lining outside its usual location.~Identify the tissue and process before learning the disease name.~Not all pelvic pain or abnormal bleeding has the same cause.
pediatrics-0~Growth changes body size; development adds abilities.|Repeated observations show the direction of change.|Losing a previously acquired skill differs from simply acquiring a new skill later.~A series of height measurements tells a story that one measurement cannot.~Growth = size; development = skills; regression = skill loss.~One point on a growth chart is not the whole assessment.
pediatrics-1~Children need nutrients both to maintain the body and to build new tissue.|Feeding assessment asks what is taken in and how growth responds.|Immunization prepares immune defenses for specific infections.~A growing building needs materials for repairs and for new rooms; children likewise have maintenance and growth needs.~Intake, absorption, needs, and growth belong together.~Enough calories alone does not guarantee all nutrient needs are met.
pediatrics-2~Before birth, the placenta supports gas and nutrient exchange.|After birth, lungs, circulation, feeding, and temperature control must adapt.|Earlier birth can leave some systems less mature.~A newborn loses heat more easily than an adult partly because of body proportions and immature control.~Newborn = changing physiology; age in hours can matter.~Newborns are not simply very small adults.
pediatrics-3~Children can show illness through feeding, activity, breathing, and fluid balance.|Age affects what findings mean.|Several observations together give a better picture than one number.~A child's breathing effort and alertness add information that temperature alone cannot provide.~Look at the whole child and the time course.~A higher fever does not always mean a more severe illness.
pediatrics-4~Some organ problems are present from birth; others develop later.|Their effects may appear in growth as well as immediate symptoms.|A kidney protein leak can lead to swelling, while a heart problem can alter blood flow.~Edema means fluid accumulating in tissues; protein loss in urine is one possible contributor.~Connect organ function to both symptoms and growth.~Congenital means present at birth, not necessarily inherited.
pediatrics-5~Children's body sizes and functions change with age.|Equipment, communication, and assessment must fit their developmental stage.|Adolescents also need space for appropriate discussion of personal concerns.~A tool sized for an adult may not fit a small child's airway or arm.~Match assessment to age, size, development, and context.~A child's needs cannot always be met by scaling down adult care.
ophthalmology-0~The cornea and lens bend light toward the retina.|The retina converts light into nerve signals.|If focus falls in the wrong place, the image is blurred.~In myopia, distant parallel rays focus in front of the retina when the eye is uncorrected.~Focus location matters: in front in myopia, behind in unaccommodated hyperopia.~Reading small letters tests only part of visual function.
ophthalmology-1~The conjunctiva covers parts of the eye surface and eyelids.|The cornea is the clear front window through which light enters.|Inflammation in these different tissues can produce different problems.~A transparent window must stay clear and smooth; corneal damage can disturb both.~Conjunctivitis affects conjunctiva; keratitis affects cornea.~All red eyes are not the same condition.
ophthalmology-2~The lens helps focus light and changes shape for near vision.|A cataract makes the lens cloudy.|Cloudiness scatters light and can reduce image clarity.~Looking through frosted glass illustrates scattering, though a cataract is inside the lens rather than on an external window.~Cataract = cloudy lens.~Cataract is not a film growing over the outside of the eye.
ophthalmology-3~The optic nerve carries visual information to the brain.|Glaucoma involves characteristic damage to this nerve.|Eye pressure is one important factor; inflammation of the uvea is a different process called uveitis.~A cable can be damaged even when one pressure measurement looks ordinary.~Glaucoma concerns nerve damage, not a pressure number alone.~Normal measured eye pressure does not rule out glaucoma.
ophthalmology-4~The retina needs healthy cells and a reliable blood supply.|Systemic diseases can affect its small vessels.|Separation from supporting tissue can also disrupt its function.~Diabetes can damage retinal vessels; retinal detachment involves separation of tissue layers. These are distinct mechanisms.~The retina is living nerve tissue, not just a passive screen.~An eye problem can reflect disease elsewhere in the body.
ophthalmology-5~Visual signals pass through the optic nerves and further brain pathways.|Different pathways carry information from different parts of the visual field.|Patterns of missing vision can help locate damage.~Losing a particular region of the visual field gives different information from a general blur.~Follow retina → nerve → brain pathway → visual cortex.~External appearance alone cannot show whether every internal eye structure is intact.
ent-0~Sound waves move the eardrum and small middle-ear bones.|The cochlea converts the resulting motion into electrical signals.|The auditory nerve carries these signals toward the brain.~A microphone system needs both sound collection and signal conversion. Hearing has similar distinct stages.~Conductive = transmission problem; sensorineural = inner-ear or nerve problem.~Hearing loss does not always mean nerve damage.
ent-1~The middle ear is an air-containing space behind the eardrum.|The eustachian tube helps connect it to the upper throat and equalize pressure.|Poor ventilation can contribute to fluid accumulation and hearing changes.~Pressure changes during travel can make the ear feel blocked, illustrating the importance of pressure balance.~Middle-ear ventilation and external ear-canal openness are different things.~Middle-ear fluid does not always mean an acute bacterial infection.
ent-2~Balance uses information from inner ears, eyes, and body-position sensors.|The brain combines these inputs.|Conflicting or abnormal signals can produce a false sense of movement called vertigo.~Feeling that the room spins while you are still illustrates vertigo; ordinary faintness is a different description.~Describe the experience before assigning a cause.~Every dizzy feeling is not vertigo, and vertigo is not always an inner-ear disease.
ent-3~The nose warms, moistens, and filters air.|Its lining can swell from allergy, infection, or other causes.|Swelling can alter drainage from the connected sinus spaces.~A narrowed drainage opening can allow fluid to accumulate without the mucus color proving a particular germ.~Rhinitis = inflamed nasal lining; epistaxis = nosebleed.~Colored discharge alone does not prove bacterial infection.
ent-4~Chewing and saliva prepare a food lump called a bolus.|Coordinated muscles move it through the throat into the food pipe.|Airway protection must be coordinated with this movement.~Swallowing difficulty and pain on swallowing can occur separately, so they have different names.~Dysphagia = difficulty; odynophagia = pain with swallowing.~The throat is not a passive tube for food to fall through.
ent-5~The larynx sits at the entrance to the lower airway.|Its vocal folds vibrate as air passes, producing voice.|It also helps protect the airway during swallowing.~A changed vibrating surface or nerve supply can alter voice quality.~Voice needs airflow, moving vocal folds, and coordinated control.~Stridor indicates narrowed airflow in the upper airway, not simply a changed voice.
orthopedics-0~A fracture is a break in bone.|Repair depends on living tissue, blood supply, and suitable stability.|New repair tissue can gradually be replaced and reshaped into stronger bone.~Like rebuilding a damaged bridge, healing needs materials and stable conditions; unlike a bridge, bone actively remodels itself.~Describe site, pattern, position, and whether it communicates with the outside.~An open fracture does not require bone to visibly stick out.
orthopedics-1~Muscles and other tissues lie within supportive connective-tissue coverings.|Swelling inside a confined space can raise pressure.|High pressure can reduce blood flow and damage tissue.~An overfilled rigid compartment has little room to expand; pressure can threaten the tissues inside.~Swelling → pressure → reduced perfusion.~A pulse can still be present early in compartment syndrome.
orthopedics-2~Microbes can infect bone or enter a joint.|The resulting immune response and infection can damage nearby structures.|A joint's smooth movement depends on surfaces that infection can rapidly disrupt.~Bone infection is osteomyelitis; infection in a joint is septic arthritis.~Locate the infection: bone or joint.~A painful swollen joint is not automatically ordinary wear and tear.
orthopedics-3~Bone contains a protein framework reinforced by minerals.|Bone strength depends on amount, arrangement, and mineral content.|Osteoporosis and osteomalacia impair strength through different mechanisms.~Too little supporting material differs from material that is inadequately hardened.~Osteoporosis: reduced strength and bone mass/structure. Osteomalacia: poor mineralization.~All fragile bones do not have the same disorder.
orthopedics-4~Growing long bones lengthen at growth plates near their ends.|An injury there can disturb future growth.|The spine is a three-dimensional structure, so deformity can involve rotation as well as sideways curvature.~Damage to one side of a growth plate may alter later alignment.~Growing tissue has consequences beyond the immediate injury.~Scoliosis is not simply a habit of standing badly.
orthopedics-5~A joint contains cartilage, lining tissue, bone, and supporting structures.|Different disorders affect different parts and patterns.|Rehabilitation aims to improve movement and participation in everyday tasks.~Being able to climb stairs again is a functional goal, beyond just changing a scan finding.~Ask what the person needs to do, not only what looks abnormal.~All arthritis is not the same as age-related joint wear.
dermatology-0~First describe a skin change by shape, height, contents, and color.|Then describe where it occurs and how it is arranged.|These features narrow possible causes but do not always settle them.~A flat patch of changed color is a macule; a small solid bump is a papule.~Flat, solid raised, or fluid-filled are useful first distinctions.~One appearance can occur in several diseases.
dermatology-1~Skin forms a barrier that helps retain water and exclude irritants.|Barrier disruption and immune activity can lead to itching and inflammation.|Different inflammatory skin diseases have different patterns and mechanisms.~Eczema often itches and involves barrier problems; psoriasis often forms clearly bounded scaly raised areas.~Link barrier function, immune response, and appearance.~A recurring rash does not automatically mean an infection.
dermatology-2~Skin organisms differ in how they interact with tissues.|Fungi can use keratin-rich skin, hair, and nails; mites can infest skin.|The cause determines which diagnostic questions matter.~Scabies is caused by a mite, not a fungus or bacterium.~Infection and infestation involve different kinds of organisms.~All itchy skin conditions are not fungal infections.
dermatology-3~Hair follicles connect to oil-producing glands.|Plugging, oil, inflammation, and microbes contribute to acne.|Melanocytes are cells that make the pigment melanin.~Pigment loss and a plugged follicle affect different skin components even if both change appearance.~Follicle = hair unit; sebum = skin oil; melanin = pigment.~Acne is not simply proof of poor hygiene.
dermatology-4~Skin layers are held together by supporting proteins.|Immune attack on these connections can allow layers to separate.|Fluid entering a separated space forms a blister.~Separating layers of a laminated sheet illustrates the location of a blister, but living skin also has inflammation and repair.~Blister level and cause are separate questions.~Every blister is not caused by friction or a burn.
dermatology-5~Some skin diseases also affect nerves or other organs.|Changes in sensation can therefore add important information to a skin examination.|Suspicious growths may need further assessment rather than recognition by appearance alone.~Leprosy can affect peripheral nerves, so a skin patch and its sensation are considered together.~Skin findings can point beyond the skin.~A person's infection or appearance does not justify stigma.
psychiatry-0~Mental-health assessment explores experiences, thinking, feelings, and daily functioning.|The time course and personal context give symptoms meaning.|The mental status examination describes current functioning alongside the history.~One poor night's sleep is different from a sustained pattern that disrupts study, relationships, and self-care.~Symptom + duration + impact + context.~A diagnosis is not a judgment of someone's character.
psychiatry-1~Mood disorders involve patterns of mood, energy, and functioning over time.|Depression is more than a brief sad feeling.|Mania includes a marked change in mood and energy with other symptoms and significant consequences.~Unusually increased activity and reduced need for sleep can be part of a larger pattern, but no single feature establishes a diagnosis.~Study the whole episode and its course.~Ordinary mood changes are not automatically bipolar disorder.
psychiatry-2~Anxiety is a threat response that can become persistent and disruptive.|Obsessions are unwanted intrusive thoughts or urges.|Compulsions are repetitive actions or mental acts linked to distress or rigid rules.~Repeated checking may temporarily reduce anxiety yet keep the cycle going; ordinary careful checking is not automatically OCD.~Trigger → distress → response → short-term relief can become a cycle.~OCD is not simply liking things tidy.
psychiatry-3~Psychosis describes disturbances in how reality is experienced or interpreted.|A hallucination is a perception-like experience without a matching external source.|Causes can include psychiatric conditions, physical illness, or substances.~Hearing a voice without an external speaker is different from mishearing an actual sound.~Describe the experience, then investigate its cause and context.~Psychosis does not mean a person has two personalities.
psychiatry-4~Attention lets a person stay engaged with the present.|Delirium causes an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness.|Dementia involves acquired cognitive decline affecting independence, usually over a different time course.~A sudden change over hours requires a different interpretation from gradual decline over months.~Timing and fluctuation are central clues.~All confusion is not dementia; the two conditions can coexist.
psychiatry-5~Children's abilities and behavior change with development.|Assessment compares patterns with developmental stage and everyday functioning.|Support can involve family, school, psychological approaches, and sometimes medication.~Difficulty in one classroom can have several explanations; observations across settings add context.~Development, environment, strengths, and difficulties belong together.~A single behavior is not enough to label a child.
radiology-0~Different imaging methods use different physical signals.|X-rays and CT use ionizing radiation; ultrasound uses sound; MRI uses magnetic fields and radio signals.|The useful method depends on the question and tissue.~A tool good for showing bone may not answer every question about soft tissue.~Choose the question before choosing the scan.~A more complex scan is not always a better scan for the task.
radiology-1~An image is a projection made under particular conditions.|Position, breathing, and exposure can change appearances.|A systematic review helps separate technical effects from possible disease.~A white area on a chest image is called an opacity, but several causes can produce it.~Quality first, anatomy next, interpretation in context.~An opacity is a description, not a diagnosis.
radiology-2~Gas, fluid, and different tissues interact differently with imaging signals.|Cross-sectional methods show internal slices rather than only an overlapping projection.|The suspected problem guides which method and view are useful.~Slices of a loaf illustrate sectional views, although scan slices are reconstructed images of a living body.~Ask which structure and which question the image addresses.~A normal scan does not exclude every possible cause of pain.
radiology-3~Brain symptoms can result from bleeding, interrupted blood supply, or other changes.|Imaging methods differ in speed and what changes they reveal.|Time since symptom onset affects what may be visible.~A very early injury can be less visible than the same injury later; interpretation needs timing.~Image + onset + examination.~An early image that looks normal does not always exclude important disease.
radiology-4~A flat image overlaps three-dimensional structures.|Different viewing angles reveal different relationships.|Soft tissues and bone marrow may require different imaging from the outer bone outline.~A crack hidden from the front may become visible from the side.~More than one view can answer a genuinely different question.~A fracture may not be obvious on the first image.
radiology-5~An imaging test should provide information useful for the clinical question.|Radiation exposure is adjusted to obtain adequate information with appropriate dose.|Contrast is a substance used to help distinguish structures, with properties depending on the method.~Making a blood vessel more visible can help trace its course, but contrast does not make every structure clearer equally.~Justify the examination and optimize how it is performed.~Contrast agents are not all the same substance.
anesthesia-0~Anesthesia changes important body functions during a procedure.|Planning considers health history, medicines, airway, and the procedure itself.|Preparation aims to anticipate how the body may respond.~The same operation can need different planning for people with different heart or breathing conditions.~Patient + procedure + expected physiological effects.~One risk score does not replace individual assessment.
anesthesia-1~Air needs an open route into the lungs.|Ventilation moves air, gas exchange transfers gases, and circulation delivers oxygen onward.|Different monitors examine different parts of this chain.~Exhaled carbon dioxide gives information about ventilation; oxygen saturation answers a different question.~Open airway → ventilation → exchange → circulation.~An oxygen reading does not directly measure how well carbon dioxide is being removed.
anesthesia-2~Unconsciousness, pain relief, and muscle immobility are separate effects.|Different drugs may provide different parts of anesthesia.|Monitoring and trained assessment follow their combined effects and recovery.~A medicine can stop muscle movement without blocking awareness or pain.~No movement does not prove no awareness or no pain.~A neuromuscular blocker alone is not a complete anesthetic.
anesthesia-3~Nerve signals depend on ions crossing membrane channels.|Local anesthetics block certain sodium channels and interrupt signal propagation.|Targeting a local area or nerve pathway can reduce sensation in that region.~Blocking a signal route differs from making the entire brain unconscious.~Channel block → interrupted conduction → reduced sensation.~Local and general anesthesia are different approaches with different effects.
anesthesia-4~A monitor measures one feature of the body, not overall health directly.|Trends and other observations help interpret each reading.|Fluid balance and organ function influence how circulation responds.~A good oxygen saturation can coexist with low blood flow, so oxygen delivery still needs broader assessment.~Measurement → meaning → context → reassessment.~One reassuring number does not guarantee every vital function is adequate.
anesthesia-5~Pain has several contributing pathways and personal dimensions.|Combining suitable approaches can address different mechanisms.|Resuscitation supports failing vital functions while trained teams assess and address causes.~Two approaches acting at different points may complement one another; combining treatments still requires clinical judgment.~Match the approach to mechanism and reassess its effect.~Knowing a written sequence is not the same as being trained to perform an emergency procedure.
`;
const BEGINNER_LESSONS = Object.fromEntries(BEGINNER_RAW.trim().split('\n').map(row => {
  const [id, story, example, remember, misconception] = row.split('~');
  return [id, {steps:story.split('|'), example, remember, misconception}];
}));
